# 4. EXPERIMENT: KEEP mu1=0, CHANGE mu2

julia -t auto
include("sweep_mu2.jl")
sweep = sweep_mu2()
save_mu2_sweep(sweep)

What it does. sweep_mu2() runs run_history_dependent_tax(; mu1 = 0, mu2 = mu2vec[i], labor_grid_size = 101) over MU2_GRID = range(0, 0.98, length = 10) and returns a NamedTuple of vectors indexed by grid point: overallValueFunction (from eq.welfare), meanAssets, medianAssets (from eq.statistics), plus theta0, lambda, govBudgetResidual, sClampedMassShare, converged, elapsedSeconds, errors. 
  
save_mu2_sweep / load_mu2_sweep follow the JLD2 convention already in run_history_dependent_tax.jl.

Note: The default λ bracket does not contain the root over the upper half of the grid. At mu2 = 0.98 the budget-clearing λ is about 0.007, against lambdaMin = 0.20 in HD_SETTINGS. This matters more than a failed run would, because the solver does not throw when the bracket has no sign change — it returns the best evaluated equilibrium. With the stock bracket that point came back looking like a normal result: λ pinned at exactly 0.200000, budget residual −0.596, and medianAssets = 11.38, which is an artifact of assets piling at the grid top. So the file does two things about it: it overrides lambdaMin = 1e-3 by default (LAMBDA_MIN_SWEEP, overridable), and it flags every point with converged[i] = abs(govBudgetResidual[i]) ≤ tolGovBudget and warns on any that fail. With the wider bracket all 10 points converge, residuals at 1e-18.

fieldnames(typeof(sweep))
(:mu2, :overallValueFunction, :meanAssets, :medianAssets, :theta0, :lambda, :govBudgetResidual, :sClampedMassShare, :converged, :elapsedSeconds, :errors, :mu1, :settings)

sweep = load_mu2_sweep("results/sweep_mu2_n=10_mu2=0.000-0.980_mu1=0.000_nS1=7_nS2=21.jld2")
